B.4   Tabular benchmarks: aloi, helena, jannis

The same closed-form identity applies feature-wise to the three tabular benchmarks, so the assumption
can be checked before training; we measure the violation on the exact features the sweep trains on,
after the min–max preprocessing used throughout the paper.
The commutator alone cannot single out jannis: helena carries a near-identical violation and
is predicted well. What distinguishes jannis is spectral degeneracy: its minimum relative gap
among the ten largest eigenvalues of ΣXX is roughly fifteen times smaller than helena’s. This
predicts the observed signature, in which per-mode identification degrades while the count-level
prediction survives: on jannis the per-mode correlation falls to +0.619, against +0.906 on helena
and +0.905 on aloi, while the signed and loose-count correlations stay at +0.648 and +0.697 with
positive confidence intervals (Table 7). With three datasets we state this as a measured, mechanism-
consistent hypothesis and not as a fitted law.

Incorporating the interaction. Write the linearised generator as J = D + ϵR, with D decoupled
and the commutator of Eq. (37) as the measurable small parameter ϵ. First-order terms shift each
µi through the diagonal of R, and modes mix through inverse-eigengap-weighted off-diagonals.
Near-degenerate blocks are therefore where interaction first affects mode identity, which is the
jannis case. Low-dimensional projectors with multiple views, as in Ghosh et al. [2024], act on
exactly that quantity, since they limit how many modes are simultaneously active and thereby shrink
the effective cross-talk; their setting is projector-based vision SSL rather than JEPA, so we take the
mechanism and not the system. We do not claim the coupled derivation here.

